[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatches found
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. Exit status on a closed stdout: C dies from SIGPIPE, Rust exited 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Status: fixed.**
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproduction — a 100×256-byte reverse (~100 KiB of stdout) piped to a reader
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that takes one byte and closes:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ c_src/build/driver   < big.txt > >(head -c 10 >/dev/null); echo $?
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 141                       # 128 + SIGPIPE(13)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ translation/.../driver < big.txt > >(head -c 10 >/dev/null); echo $?
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 0                         # before the fix
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** The Rust standard library installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` before
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` runs. The C program keeps the platform default, so a `write` to a pipe
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m with no reader kills it with signal 13. In Rust the same `write` returned
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `EPIPE`, the error was discarded, and the process exited normally. The output
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m bytes and stderr agreed; only the wait status diverged.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is reachable for real: any output-producing operation (`OP_REVERSE`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `OP_ROTATE`, `OP_CHECKSUM`, `OP_SPLIT`, `OP_COPY`, `OP_MERGE`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `OP_INTERLEAVE`) writes enough to fill a pipe buffer at maximum scale.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/main.rs` now restores the default disposition as the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m first statement of `main`, via a direct `extern "C" { fn signal(...) }`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m declaration so no new dependency is introduced:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fn main() {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     sigpipe::restore_default();   // signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     ...
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Covered by `closed_stdout_kills_both_the_same_way`. That test was confirmed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m non-vacuous: commenting out the call makes it fail with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `C signal: 13 (SIGPIPE) vs Rust exit status: 0`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors that look like bugs and were deliberately preserved
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each of these was checked against the C rather than "corrected". They were
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m already right in the translation; they are listed so the next reader can
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m re-check them instead of assuming.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`scanf("%d")` spans newlines.** The input has no line structure. `1 1 3 1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   2 3` and one token per line produce identical output. All six C `isspace`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   bytes are separators, including `\v` (0x0b) and `\f` (0x0c).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`scanf_crosses_newlines_and_accepts_all_whitespace`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **Integer overflow truncates, it does not saturate.** glibc converts the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   digits to a `long` (saturating at `LONG_MIN`/`LONG_MAX`) and then stores the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   low 32 bits into the `int`. So `2147483648` becomes `-2147483648`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `4294967296` becomes `0` (i.e. `OP_COPY`), and `4294967298` is an accepted
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   buffer count of `2`. A saturating implementation is observably wrong here.
